63:This study aims to construct growth curve models to characterize the morphological  features  of  the  corpus  callosum  in  children  aged  1 -6 years. By elucidating the growth rate and inter-individual variability of ten  fine-grained  morphometrics  of  the  corpus  callosum,  we  seek  to provide insights into the timing and pace of corpus callosum maturation during early childhood. Furthermore, we aim to examine the hypothesis that the these growth curve models are effective in identifying atypical development associated ASD. If the hypothesis is proved, this approach could lead to a novel tool for quantitatively recognizing corpus callosum abnormalities and assessing developmental deviations.
71:This study utilized two brain imaging datasets collected at Zhejiang Children ' s Hospital, Zhejiang University. Dataset 1 consists of structural brain images from 295 typically developing children (TDC), aged 1 -6 years, and was used to construct growth curve models of the corpus callosum. Dataset 2 includes brain images from 26 children with ASD and 41 TDC children. Dataset 2 was used to assess the capability of the growth  curve  models  to  identify  ASD  based  on  deviations  in  corpus callosum morphology. This study is approved by The Medical Research Ethics Committee at the Children ' s Hospital. The parents of all participants signed informed consent form.
